# Copy this file to `.kamal/secrets` before the first deploy.
#
#     cp .kamal/secrets.example .kamal/secrets
#
# `.kamal/secrets` is in .gitignore. This example isn't: it contains no secret,
# it just says where to get them. Where the deploy *goes* — the host, the user,
# the music — is a separate file: see `.kamal/deploy.env.example`.
#
# The registry runs on the server itself, on your LAN, with no real auth, so
# this password is a formality, not a secret: Kamal refuses a non-localhost
# registry without one, and the registry accepts anything. It stays here in the
# clear on purpose.
KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD=kamal
#
# The master key lives in config/master.key, which is gitignored. If you cloned
# the repo onto a new machine, copy it from one that already has it; if this is
# a fresh install of your own, `bin/rails credentials:edit` writes you one.
# Without it, the app won't boot in production.
RAILS_MASTER_KEY=$(cat config/master.key)
#
# The certificate Mediateca is served under, and its key — only when you set
# MEDIATECA_TLS_HOST. Kamal reads them from here and hands them to kamal-proxy;
# they never reach the app. Both, or neither: Kamal refuses one without the other.
#
# The README has the three commands that make them. Whatever you do with the
# certificate, the *key* is a private key: keep it out of the repo, and prefer a
# keychain to a file on disk.
#
# On a Mac, that means putting them there once —
#
#     security add-generic-password -a mediateca -s mediateca-tls-cert -w "$(cat mediateca.pem)"
#     security add-generic-password -a mediateca -s mediateca-tls-key  -w "$(cat mediateca-key.pem)"
#
# — and reading them back here:
#
# MEDIATECA_TLS_CERT=$(security find-generic-password -a mediateca -s mediateca-tls-cert -w)
# MEDIATECA_TLS_KEY=$(security find-generic-password -a mediateca -s mediateca-tls-key -w)
#
# Anywhere else, read them from files you keep outside the repo:
#
# MEDIATECA_TLS_CERT=$(cat ~/.mediateca/tls/cert.pem)
# MEDIATECA_TLS_KEY=$(cat ~/.mediateca/tls/key.pem)
